.TH AVR\-SERVICE\-TOKEN\-CREATE 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-service-token-create \- Create a service token and print its secret once.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr service-token create
[OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a service token and print its secret once.
.PP
The token belongs to the group --group names, not to you, and keeps working when you leave the organization. It needs at least one grant or a VM create limit. Repository and firewall-rule grants are reads; a VM grant is read or write. Each grant must be within your own access now. A token with a VM create limit creates VMs and administers the ones it created; a token of the root group cannot have one. The secret is shown once and cannot be retrieved later: store it, for example as a CI secret, and set it as AVR_TOKEN where the token is used. The API may ask for a recent sign-in; the command then exits 4 and says to sign in again.
.PP
--group takes a group ID or a group's path as `avr group list` shows it, such as Organization/Platform/CI. Names compare without case. A value equal to one group's whole path selects that group; otherwise the end of a path, such as Platform/CI or a group name like CI, must select exactly one group.
.PP
With --json, the selection must include the token field, because the secret cannot be read again. If a --jq filter fails or drops the secret, the command prints the unfiltered JSON instead and exits nonzero, so the secret is not lost.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Examples:
    avr service-token create --name ci --group Platform/CI --repo acme/api
    avr service-token create --name egress --group CI --all-firewall-rules --ttl 7d
    avr service-token create --name agents --group Platform/CI --repo acme/api --vm-create-limit 5
    avr service-token create --name ci --group <group-id> --repo acme/api --json token --jq .token
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
JSON FIELDS
    blocked_by, created_at, created_by, expires_at, expiring_soon, grants,
    grants_version, group_id, id, last_used_at, name, organization_id,
    previous_valid_until, revoked_at, revoked_reason, rotated_at,
    rotation_version, status, vm_cap, vm_count, token
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fITEXT\fR
Token name (1-100 characters). (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-group\fR \fITEXT\fR
The group that owns the token: its name, path or ID (grp-xxx). (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR, \fB\-R\fR \fITEXT\fR
Grant read access to a repository (org/repo or rep-xxx). Repeatable. (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-firewall\-rule\fR \fITEXT\fR
Grant read access to an egress firewall rule, by rule ID (efr-xxx). Repeatable. (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-firewall\-rules\fR
Grant read access to every egress firewall rule, including rules added later.
.TP
\fB\-\-vm\fR \fITEXT\fR
Grant access to a VM, by VM ID (cvm-xxx): read, or write with cvm-xxx:write. Repeatable. (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\-vms\fR \fICHOICE\fR
Grant read or write access to every VM of the organization, including VMs created later. (choices: read, write)
.TP
\fB\-\-vm\-create\-limit\fR \fIINTEGER RANGE\fR
Let the token create VMs while it owns fewer than this many. A token of the root group cannot have a limit.
.TP
\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fITEXT\fR
Lifetime: e.g. 30m, 8h, 7d, or a number of seconds. 60 seconds to 90 days; the server default is 30 days.
.TP
\fB\-\-never\-expires\fR
The secret never expires: anyone holding it keeps its access until it is revoked. Cannot be combined with --ttl.
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID or slug. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR \fITEXT\fR
Output JSON. Pass comma-separated field names, "*" for all fields, or "?" to list available fields.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-jq\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter --json output through a jq expression.
.SH SEE ALSO
